chitectural decision in your learning records.\n\nReady to start **Lesson 0001: 2.5D Isometric Math & Coordinates**? We will look at how isometric projections work and write the fundamental grid-to-screen conversion functions in Rust.","thinking":"Considering the user's situation, building a game engine from scratch is a significant undertaking, particularly for someone new to game development, which it seems they are. The existence of C++ code might seem to suggest an advantage to building, but the effort required to port, maintain, and expand a custom engine from scratch is considerable compared to leveraging an existing solution. The existing code's nature and quality, along with the project's complexity, must be evaluated.\n\n\nBuilding from existing C++ code might seem straightforward initially, but direct translation to Rust presents significant challenges due to fundamental differences in memory management and architecture. C++'s reliance on inheritance, cyclical pointers, and shared mutable state clashes heavily with Rust's ownership and borrowing rules. A line-by-line port would likely result in unsafe, inefficient code, negating Rust's safety benefits. Instead of attempting a direct port, consider exploring a solution that provides a robust and naturally Rust-friendly architecture.\n\n\nFocus shifts to how existing C++ code can be leveraged with a modern engine. Rendering and windowing systems are not suitable for porting; they should be replaced. Valuable assets for porting include data formats, game rules, mathematical formulas, and communication protocols. Bevy's architecture facilitates this significantly.\n\nA completely new engine makes sense only if the objective is to deeply understand low-level graphics programming or to customize a rendering pipeline extensively.\n\n\nVerdict: **Use Bevy or a similar existing framework; do NOT build a custom engine.** The scope of a 2.5D MMORPG is already immense. Creating an engine concurrently would severely limit the project's feasibility.\n\nLeveraging existing C++ code might seem beneficial, but direct translation to Rust would be counterproductive, particularly regarding memory management and Rust's ownership model. Instead, treat the C++ code as a source of game logic, not engine components.\n\nConsider the existing C++ code's valuable parts to be ported: data formats, game rules, and network protocols. Replace the engine elements such as rendering and windowing, not port them. Bevy's architecture fits this approach well.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
